Part How it works <code>intact</code> The normal model, with all its LODs (<code>models/x.obj</code> or <code>original:MESH</code>); <code>scale</code> converts model units to metres. <code>textures</code> Swaps always applied: <code>texture name: replacement</code>. <code>damage</code> A list of levels, each <code>{below: &lt;hull fraction&gt;, textures: {…}}</code>. Every level the hull is below applies, in order, so the worst level wins where two swap the same texture. <code>states</code> Named conditions, each with <code>textures</code> swaps, optionally its own <code>model</code> (a mesh replacing the intact one in that state; collision keeps the intact shape) and its own <code>debris</code> set: construction stages (<code>build_25</code>, <code>build_60</code>, <code>build_90</code>), <code>destroyed</code> (see below). A scenario object shows a state with <code>state:</code>. <code>debris</code> The debris set thrown when the object is destroyed. <code>destroyed_effect</code> The effect of its explosion (default: the engine's <code>explosion</code>). <p>Texture names. A swap matches a model's texture by name: the file name without folders or extension, compared case-insensitively (<code>texture_key</code>). <code>guard_hull</code> matches <code>../textures/guard_hull.png</code> in an OBJ's <code>.mtl</code>; <code>inttop</code> matches the original texture <code>INTTOP</code>. So a modder's damage textures need no change to the model itself.</p> <p>Destroyed in place. A station module whose model set has a <code>destroyed</code> state does not vanish when it is destroyed: it stays mounted on its station as a wreck drawn with the <code>destroyed</code> swaps (for example a blackened turret), until the station itself goes (Stations and modules).</p> <p>The renderer loads each distinct combination of swaps once and caches it, so damage levels cost nothing per frame.</p> <p>Construction stages. The default campaign's facilities (mining facility, research station, manufacturing center) and the Guard station have three construction stages, each a state with its own mesh and debris:</p> <pre><code>modelsets:
  mining_facility:
    intact: models/mining_facility.obj
    states:
      build_25: {model: models/mining_facility_build1.obj, debris: mining_facility_build1}
      build_60: {model: models/mining_facility_build2.obj, debris: mining_facility_build2}
      build_90: {model: models/mining_facility_build3.obj, debris: mining_facility_build3}
</code></pre> <p>A scenario places a station in a stage with <code>state: build_60</code>; an object placed in a state has no modules and no point defence (it is not finished). The strategy layer will pick the stage from build progress (Logistics). <code>--check</code> loads every state mesh, and the viewer's D shows them.</p>
